          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My son has been wheezing at night for a month and his blue inhaler is running out fast. Should he be seen urgently, and what does asthma guidance describe about stepping up treatment?",
          "answer": "Your son's symptoms need a doctor's review soon. The emergency check did not flag an emergency, but that is not a clearance. Get help right away if he has any of these signs:\n\n- Trouble speaking in full sentences, or pausing for breath between words\n- Breathlessness at rest, or breathing that comes on suddenly\n- Blue or grey lips, tongue, face, or fingertips\n- Drowsiness, confusion, or exhaustion\n- Fast breathing, skin sucking in between or under the ribs, grunting, or flaring nostrils\n- A chest that has gone quiet, or no improvement after his reliever inhaler\n\nThese signs are listed in the \"Severe difficulty breathing\" guidance. The \"Asthma\" summary adds that an attack which does not respond to the reliever, or which returns within a few hours, needs prompt medical assessment.\n\nOn treatment, the asthma guideline note (\"Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach to controller therapy\") says treatment is organised in steps. The step is chosen from current symptom control and past flare-ups, and it is reviewed rather than fixed. Before stepping up, the guideline says a clinician should check inhaler technique and whether the medicine is being taken as prescribed. It notes these often explain apparent treatment failure. The \"Sticking with asthma treatment\" guidance adds that rising use of a reliever inhaler is a sign the condition may not be well controlled. It also says a written action plan helps families know what to do when symptoms worsen.\n\nThe documents I searched do not say how quickly a child with this pattern should be seen, and they do not say which medicine or dose would suit him. A clinician needs to assess him and decide on any change to his treatment. Until then, keep the reliever to hand and use the signs above to decide when to get emergency help.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
